          "type": "turn",
          "question": "When a code must never be sequenced first, how is that shown in the classification?",
          "answer": "The retrieved passages explain the convention in general terms, but they do not show how it is printed in the code book.\n\n**What the corpus says.** The code-structure guidance (coding-icd10-cm-code-structure-and-conventions) describes \"code first\" and \"use additional code\" as a pair that fixes sequencing: the underlying condition is reported before its manifestation. It also describes \"code also,\" which means two codes are needed but does not set an order. The guidance does not spell out a \"never sequence first\" rule in those words, so I cannot confirm the exact wording of that rule from these passages.\n\n**What it does not say.** None of the retrieved passages describe the visual markers used in the code book, such as italics, square brackets, daggers or asterisks, or any other symbol that flags a manifestation code. I searched for these terms and found nothing, so I cannot tell you how a particular edition marks them. Printed code books and the official tabular list are the places to check that notation.\n\n**Examples from the corpus.** The diabetes guidance says a combination code names the association, and an additional code carries the detail, such as the stage of kidney disease. The anemia guidance notes that some blood codes are manifestations whose sequencing instructions reach into other chapters.\n\nIf you are working on a specific code, a trained medical coder or your local coding reference can confirm the sequencing rule and its markings. This is general education, not guidance for a particular record.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
